.\" Written from docs/index.html by go run ./tools/man; edit the manual, not this.
.TH CONN 1 "September 2026" "conn 0.11.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conn \- a station for one operator directing many processes on one machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.br
.B conn down
.br
.B conn theme claude
.br
.B conn theme vim
.br
.B conn \-\-light
.br
.B conn \-\-dark
.br
.B conn \-\-help
.br
.B conn \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
The station shows the shells, contacts, and commands running in each project,
what each is doing, and which one is waiting for the operator. macOS and Linux,
on tmux.
.SH GENERAL
.TP
1-1.
conn is a station for one operator directing many processes on one machine. It
runs on tmux, which it starts and holds. The panel occupies the left of the
terminal and the workspace the rest. The panel lists the processes working each
project — shells, contacts, and commands — with what each is doing and which
is waiting on the operator. The workspace holds the one process the operator is
in. Keys move between them.
.TP
1-2.
A contact is a process with an agent at the other end: Claude Code, or another
conn knows by name. A contact works without direction and stops to ask for it.
The station shows which one has stopped, and \fBtab\fR goes to the one that has
waited longest.
.TP
1-3.
conn finds projects by walking the roots it was given: the directories named in
\fB~/.config/conn/config.json\fR, or in \fBCONN_ROOTS\fR, which is read first. A
project is a repository under a root, or a folder under one that holds two or
more. conn is told the roots and infers nothing; a conn that has been told
nothing asks before it goes on, in a line typed into: the directories that
answer what is typed are the rows, \fBtab\fR fills the line in with the one
under the cursor, and \fBenter\fR takes it and writes it to the file.
.TP
1-4.
The installation procedure on the cover puts the binary in \fB~/.local/bin\fR
and this manual beside it, where \fBman conn\fR finds it. conn needs tmux on the
path. On macOS it also needs lsof, which is how it reads where a process is
working; Linux reads /proc. Without tmux, conn shows the console and the
processes view and can reach nothing.
.SH OPERATION
.TP
2-1.
conn comes up on the console: a readout of the machine and the session, the
start-up checks, and the verdict. A check that found something wrong is a fault,
and the verdict counts them; with none it says so. Any key continues to the
processes view; from there \fBc\fR brings the console back.
.TP
2-2.
The processes view is what is running, by project. Each project work is
happening in is a block, and each process there is a row, nested under whatever
runs it. A row says its kind, what it is doing, how long it has stood as it
does, and the word for how it stands. The projects are in the order of their
paths, as the list has them. Within one, nothing moves once placed: what is new
goes on the end. A row conn holds in a pane of its server is written in the ink
and can be entered; work conn can only report is dimmed.
.PP
.I "Kinds"
.RS
.TP
\fBSHELL\fR
A shell. Bare, it is idle; running anything, it is active as what it runs is.
.TP
\fBCONTACT\fR
An agent, and the one kind that can wait on the operator.
.TP
\fBEDITOR\fR
An editor.
.TP
\fBRUN\fR
Anything else: a build, a test, a server, a script.
.TP
\fBSERVICE\fR
A docker container, filed under the project compose started it for.
.RE
.PP
.I "Words"
.RS
.TP
\fBWORKING\fR
Doing something now. A contact says what: the tool it has in flight.
.TP
\fBWAITING\fR
A contact stopped on a question it put to the operator.
.TP
\fBACTIVE\fR
Alive, and not doing anything.
.TP
\fBIDLE\fR
A shell at its prompt, or a contact at rest.
.TP
\fBSTOPPED\fR
Suspended.
.TP
\fBENDED\fR
Finished, and not yet collected.
.TP
\fBDOWN\fR
Declared in the project's .conn, and not running.
.TP
\fBEXIT n\fR
Ended with that code: a service, or a declared process. A fault.
.TP
\fBUNHEALTHY, RESTARTING, STARTING\fR
A service, as docker says it stands. The first two are faults.
.RE
.TP
2-3.
While the keys are on the panel, the workspace holds the readout on the row
under the cursor: the whole command, where the process is working, what runs it
and what it runs, what it has spent, what a contact is stopped on, and what git
says of the project. It follows the cursor. \fBenter\fR puts the process in the
workspace instead, with the keys in it, and \fBesc\fR goes back into the process
last worked in.
.TP
2-4.
\fBp\fR is the projects list: every project under the roots, whether anything is
running in it or not, and under each what conn has running there. Typing narrows
it. The workspace holds the readout here too, on the row under the cursor: a
process's own, or for a project where it is, what git says of it, and what is
running in it. \fBenter\fR opens a shell at the row under the cursor,
\fBalt\-c\fR starts a contact there, \fBalt\-a\fR lists the sessions a contact
left suspended there, \fBalt\-u\fR brings up what the project declares, and
\fBesc\fR goes back. In the sessions list, \fBenter\fR picks the session under
the cursor back up.
.TP
2-5.
\fBx\fR asks to end the process under the cursor, on the status line. \fBx\fR,
\fBy\fR or \fBenter\fR answers yes and any other key withdraws the question. A
bare shell is killed outright; anything a shell runs is sent SIGTERM; a service
is stopped with docker stop. The shell a process ran in is left at its prompt.
.TP
2-6.
A service has no pane, so \fBenter\fR on one opens its output, and \fBenter\fR
again goes into the pane holding that; \fBs\fR on one opens a shell inside the
container.
.TP
2-7.
A project may declare the processes that work it in a file named \fB.conn\fR at
its path; the file's form is at 4-5. A project with one has a block whether or
not anything is running in it, and each declared process not running is a row at
the foot of the block, dimmed and worded DOWN. \fBenter\fR on such a row brings
it up in a pane of the station and goes into it; \fBu\fR on any row of the
project brings up everything it declares that is down. A pane conn opened this
way carries the declared name on its head row. When the process ends the pane
holds its output and the row reads ENDED, or EXIT and the code as a fault;
\fBenter\fR goes in to read it, \fBu\fR opens it anew, and \fBx\fR closes it. A
file that will not read is said under the block, with the line.
.SH KEYS
.TP
3-1.
A chord is the prefix — \fBctrl\-space\fR, or what \fBCONN_PREFIX\fR names —
followed by one key. A chord works from anywhere in the station, including from
inside a process, because tmux takes it before the process does. The status line
says PREFIX while one is pending.
.PP
.I "Chords"
.RS
.TP
\fBprefix \-\fR
The processes view, from wherever the keys are. Puts the manual away if it is
up.
.TP
\fBprefix p\fR
The projects list.
.TP
\fBprefix prefix\fR
The process worked in before this one.
.TP
\fBprefix tab\fR
The process that has waited longest.
.TP
\fBprefix j\fR
Down the processes conn can put in the workspace.
.TP
\fBprefix k\fR
Up them.
.TP
\fBprefix s\fR
A shell at the project the panel is looking at.
.TP
\fBprefix a\fR
A contact at that project.
.TP
\fBprefix alt\-a\fR
The sessions suspended at that project.
.TP
\fBprefix ?\fR
This manual, in the workspace, with the keys in it.
.TP
\fBprefix q\fR
Detach. The station keeps running.
.RE
.TP
3-2.
The panel's own keys work while the panel has them, and are listed here as the
processes view takes them. In the projects list and the sessions list a letter
is typed into the line instead, and the line is edited with readline's keys:
there, \fBenter\fR takes the row under the cursor and \fBesc\fR goes back.
.PP
.I "The panel"
.RS
.TP
\fBj, k\fR
Down and up the rows.
.TP
\fBg g\fR
The first row.
.TP
\fBG\fR
The last row.
.TP
\fBM\fR
The middle row.
.TP
\fBenter\fR
Into the process under the cursor. On a service, its output; enter again goes
into the pane holding it. On a declared process that is down, brings it up.
.TP
\fBesc\fR
Back into the process last worked in.
.TP
\fBs\fR
A shell at the project. On a service, a shell inside the container.
.TP
\fBa\fR
A contact at the project.
.TP
\fBx\fR
End it: a signal to a process, docker stop to a service, and on a declared
process that has ended, close its pane. Asks first.
.TP
\fBu\fR
Bring up what the project declares and does not have running.
.TP
\fBtab\fR
The process that has waited longest.
.TP
\fBp\fR
The projects list.
.TP
\fBc\fR
The console.
.TP
\fBq\fR
Detach.
.RE
.TP
3-3.
While this manual is up the keys are in it, and the status line says HELP. It
scrolls with \fBj\fR and \fBk\fR, a screen at a time with \fBspace\fR and
\fBb\fR, and to either end with \fBg\fR and \fBG\fR. \fBesc\fR leaves it and
puts the keys back where they were when it was asked for — in the workspace,
or on the panel; \fBprefix \-\fR leaves it for the processes view whichever it
was.
.SH COMMANDS AND CONFIGURATION
.TP
4-1.
conn is called as follows. A second conn, run while the station is up, attaches
to it.
.RS
.TP
\fBconn\fR
The console, then the processes view.
.TP
\fBconn down\fR
Take the server down, with everything in it.
.TP
\fBconn theme claude\fR
Write conn's palette as a theme for Claude Code, which draws its own colors.
.TP
\fBconn theme vim\fR
Write it as a colorscheme for nvim, which does the same.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-light\fR
Say the ground is light, for a server coming up or one already up.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-dark\fR
Say the ground is dark.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-help\fR
Say this.
.TP
\fBconn \-\-version\fR
Say the build.
.RE
.TP
4-2.
conn comes up dark, or light when the terminal says its own ground is light. The
choice is made when the server comes up and holds for its life; \fBconn down\fR
and conn again asks afresh. Everything in a pane of the server draws in conn's
palette by asking the terminal for a color by name. Claude Code and nvim write
their own hex instead, and \fBconn theme\fR writes each a theme from the same
palette.
.TP
4-3.
The configuration file is \fB~/.config/conn/config.json\fR. It holds one key.
.RS
.TP
\fBroots\fR
The directories conn looks for projects under, as a list of paths. A leading ~
is the home.
.RE
.TP
4-4.
The environment says what one run of conn should do differently from what the
file says is usual.
.RS
.TP
\fBCONN_ROOTS\fR
The roots for this run, separated the way PATH is. Read ahead of the file.
.TP
\fBCONN_PREFIX\fR
The prefix, in tmux's spelling. C-Space unless set.
.TP
\fBCONN_SOCKET\fR
The socket of the server, for a second station on one machine. Under
~/.local/state/conn unless set.
.RE
.TP
4-5.
A project's \fB.conn\fR declares the processes that work it, one a line, as
\fBname [dir]: command\fR. A line beginning with # is a comment. A line that
will not parse is said under the project's block with its number, and nothing is
guessed at in its place.
.RS
.TP
\fBname\fR
One word of letters, digits, dots, dashes and underscores, and the row's label.
.TP
\fBdir\fR
Where the pane opens, under the project. Left out, the project itself.
.TP
\fBcommand\fR
A shell line, run as written.
.RE
.PP
.RS
.nf
# .conn
web frontend: npm run dev
api: go run ./cmd/api
worker services/queue: make run
.fi
.RE
